WEEK 3 – JAVASCRIPT AND TYPESCRIPT

During Week 3, I continued studying JavaScript and covered more advanced concepts that build on the fundamentals from Week 2. I learned about functions in more detail, including arrow functions, callback functions, default parameters, rest parameters, and the spread operator. I also studied promises, asynchronous JavaScript, async and await, DOM manipulation, events, form handling, and JavaScript modules. I then began TypeScript, learning why types are useful, basic type annotations, interfaces, type aliases, union types, optional properties, arrays, generics, function types, and how to understand TypeScript error messages.

I studied arrow functions, which provide a shorter syntax for writing functions in JavaScript. An arrow function uses the => symbol instead of the function keyword. For example, ‘const add = (a, b) => { return a + b; };’. If the function contains only one expression, the curly brackets and return statement can be omitted, giving ‘const add = (a, b) => a + b;’. Arrow functions can also have one parameter without parentheses, for example ‘const square = number => number * number;’. However, parentheses are required when there are no parameters, such as ‘const greet = () => console.log(‘Hello!’);’. Arrow functions are commonly used with array methods such as map(), filter(), and reduce(), as well as with callbacks and event handlers.

I learned about callback functions. A callback is a function that is passed as an argument to another function and is then called by that function. For example, ’function greet(name) { console.log(‘Hello ’ + name); }’ and ‘function processUser(callback) { callback(‘Denise’); }’. Calling ‘processUser(greet);’ passes the greet function to processUser, where it is executed. Callbacks are useful because they allow one function to specify what should happen when another function completes a task. They are commonly used in array methods, events, timers, and asynchronous JavaScript. For example, ‘setTimeout(() => { console.log(‘Hello’); }, 2000);’ uses an arrow function as a callback that will run after two seconds.

I studied default parameters, which allow a function parameter to have a default value when no argument is provided. For example, ’function greet(name = ‘Guest’) { console.log(‘Hello ’ + name); }’. If ‘greet(‘Denise’);’ is called, the value Denise is used, but if ‘greet();’ is called, the default value Guest is used. Default parameters are useful when a function should have a reasonable value to use when the caller does not provide one. A default parameter is normally used when the corresponding argument is undefined.

I also studied rest parameters. Rest parameters use three dots (…) before the parameter name and allow a function to accept any number of arguments. For example, ‘function add(…numbers) { let total = 0; for (let number of numbers) { total += number; } return total; }’. Calling ‘add(1, 2, 3, 4);’ allows all the numbers to be collected into the numbers array. Rest parameters are useful when the number of arguments is not known in advance. The rest parameter must be the last parameter in a function. For example, ‘function showName(firstName, lastName, …titles) { }’ is valid because titles is the last parameter.

I learned about the spread operator, which also uses three dots (…), but works differently from the rest parameter. The spread operator expands the elements of an array or the properties of an object. For example, if ‘let numbers = [1, 2, 3];’ is declared, ‘let newNumbers = […numbers, 4, 5];’ creates a new array containing 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The spread operator can also be used to combine arrays, such as ‘let combined = […first, …second];’. It can also be used to copy objects or add new properties, for example ‘let updatedUser = { …user, age: 25 };’. The main difference is that rest collects multiple values into one array, while spread expands the contents of an array or object.

I continued studying array methods and learned more about how higher-order functions work with arrays. The forEach() method executes a function for every element in an array but does not normally create a new array. For example, ‘numbers.forEach(number => console.log(number));’ prints every number. The map() method creates a new array by transforming every element. For example, ‘let doubled = numbers.map(number => number * 2);’. The filter() method creates a new array containing only elements that satisfy a condition. For example, ‘let evenNumbers = numbers.filter(number => number % 2 === 0);’. The find() method returns the first element that satisfies a condition, such as ‘let result = numbers.find(number => number > 10);’. The some() method checks whether at least one element satisfies a condition, while every() checks whether all elements satisfy a condition. For example, ‘numbers.some(number => number > 10);’ returns true if at least one number is greater than 10, while ‘numbers.every(number => number > 0);’ returns true only if every number is greater than 0.

I studied the reduce() method in more detail. The reduce() method processes all the elements of an array and combines them into a single value. For example, ‘let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];’ and ‘let total = numbers.reduce((sum, current) => sum + current, 0);’. The result is 10. The second argument, 0, is the initial value of the accumulator. reduce() can be used for calculations such as finding totals, counting items, or combining data from an array into another structure.

I learned about Promises in JavaScript. A Promise is an object that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation. A Promise can have three main states: pending, fulfilled, or rejected. Pending means the operation has not yet finished. Fulfilled means the operation completed successfully, while rejected means the operation failed. A Promise can be created using ‘new Promise()’. For example, ‘const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => { const success = true; if (success) { resolve(‘Operation successful’); } else { reject(‘Operation failed’); } });’. The resolve() function is used when the operation succeeds, while reject() is used when the operation fails.

I learned how to handle the result of a Promise using then() and catch(). The then() method is used when the Promise is successfully fulfilled. For example, ‘promise.then(result => { console.log(result); });’. The catch() method handles an error when the Promise is rejected. For example, ‘promise.catch(error => { console.log(error); });’. They can also be combined, such as ‘promise.then(result => { console.log(result); }).catch(error => { console.log(error); });’. This allows JavaScript programs to handle successful and unsuccessful asynchronous operations.

I studied asynchronous JavaScript and learned why asynchronous programming is important. JavaScript normally executes code from top to bottom, but some operations take time to complete, such as loading data from a server, reading a file, or waiting for a timer. Asynchronous programming allows JavaScript to continue performing other tasks instead of stopping the entire program while waiting. Promises are one of the main ways JavaScript handles asynchronous operations.

I learned about async and await, which provide a cleaner way of working with Promises. An async function is declared using the async keyword and automatically returns a Promise. The await keyword is used inside an async function to wait for a Promise to settle before continuing with the next line. For example, ‘async function getData() { const result = await fetchData(); console.log(result); }’. Using async and await often makes asynchronous code easier to read because it looks more like normal sequential code. Errors in async and await code can be handled using try and catch, for example ‘try { const result = await getData(); } catch (error) { console.log(error); }’.

I studied the Document Object Model, commonly called the DOM. The DOM represents an HTML document as objects that JavaScript can access and modify. JavaScript can use the DOM to change HTML content, modify styles, create elements, remove elements, and respond to user actions. For example, if an HTML page contains ‘Hello’, JavaScript can select the element using ‘let message = document.getElementById(‘message’);’ and change its content using ‘message.textContent = ‘Hello Denise’;’. This allows JavaScript to make web pages dynamic and interactive.

I learned different ways of selecting HTML elements from the DOM. The getElementById() method selects an element using its id. For example, ‘document.getElementById(‘message’);’. I also learned about querySelector(), which can select an element using a CSS selector. For example, ‘document.querySelector(’#message’);’ selects an element with the id message, while ‘document.querySelector(’.button’);’ selects the first element with the button class. querySelectorAll() can be used to select multiple elements that match a CSS selector.

I studied how JavaScript can change HTML content. The textContent property changes or reads the text inside an element. For example, ‘document.querySelector(’#message’).textContent = ‘Welcome’;’. I also learned about innerHTML, which allows HTML content to be inserted inside an element. For example, ‘document.querySelector(’#container’).innerHTML = ‘Hello’;’. textContent is useful when only text is needed, while innerHTML allows HTML markup to be inserted.

I learned that JavaScript can also modify the style of HTML elements. For example, ‘let message = document.querySelector(’#message’);’ followed by ‘message.style.color = ‘blue’;’ changes the text color. Other CSS properties can also be changed through the style property. JavaScript can therefore change the appearance of a webpage while the page is running.

I studied JavaScript events. An event is an action that occurs in a webpage, usually caused by the user or the browser. Common events include click, submit, input, change, mouseover, keydown, and keyup. JavaScript can respond to events using addEventListener(). For example, ‘let button = document.querySelector(’#button’);’ and ‘button.addEventListener(‘click’, () => { console.log(‘Button clicked’); });’. In this example, the arrow function is executed whenever the button is clicked.

I learned about event objects. When an event occurs, JavaScript can provide information about that event through an event object. For example, ‘button.addEventListener(‘click’, event => { console.log(event); });’. The event object can provide information such as the element that triggered the event and the type of event that occurred. I also learned about preventDefault(), which prevents the browser from performing its normal default action. For example, when handling a form submission, ‘event.preventDefault();’ can prevent the page from automatically reloading or submitting the form in the usual way.

I studied form handling in JavaScript. Forms allow users to enter information such as usernames, passwords, names, email addresses, and other data. JavaScript can access the values entered into form fields using the value property. For example, if an input has the id username, JavaScript can use ‘let username = document.querySelector(’#username’).value;’. A submit event can be handled using addEventListener(). For example, ‘form.addEventListener(‘submit’, event => { event.preventDefault(); let username = document.querySelector(’#username’).value; console.log(username); });’. This allows JavaScript to process form information without necessarily allowing the browser to reload the page.

I learned about JavaScript modules. Modules allow a program to be divided into separate files so that code can be organized and reused. JavaScript modules commonly use the export and import keywords. For example, a function can be exported from a file using ‘export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }’. Another JavaScript file can import it using ‘import { add } from ‘./math.js’;’. Modules are useful for larger applications because they prevent all the code from being placed in one large file and make it easier to organize and maintain a project.

I then began studying TypeScript. TypeScript is a programming language built on JavaScript that adds static typing and other features to JavaScript. TypeScript code is eventually converted or compiled into JavaScript so that it can run in environments that support JavaScript. The main difference is that JavaScript normally determines types while the program is running, whereas TypeScript allows developers to specify and check types while writing the program. For example, JavaScript can have ‘let age = 20;’, while TypeScript can have ‘let age: number = 20;’.

I learned why types are useful in TypeScript. A type tells TypeScript what kind of value a variable is expected to contain. For example, ‘let name: string = ‘Denise’;’ means name should contain a string, while ‘let age: number = 20;’ means age should contain a number. If I later write ‘age = ‘twenty’;’, TypeScript can report an error because a string was assigned to a variable that was declared as a number. Types therefore help detect certain mistakes before the program is executed.

I studied basic TypeScript type annotations. A type annotation is written after the variable name using a colon. For example, ‘let name: string = ‘Denise’;’, ‘let age: number = 20;’, and ‘let isStudent: boolean = true;’. TypeScript supports common types such as string, number, and boolean. Arrays can also be typed, for example ‘let numbers: number[] = [1, 2, 3];’ and ‘let names: string[] = [‘John’, ‘Mary’, ‘Peter’];’. Another way to write an array type is ‘let numbers: Array = [1, 2, 3];’.

I learned about type inference. TypeScript can sometimes automatically determine the type of a variable based on the value assigned to it. For example, when writing ‘let age = 20;’, TypeScript can infer that age is a number. Similarly, ‘let name = ‘Denise’;’ allows TypeScript to infer that name is a string. Because of type inference, it is not necessary to explicitly write a type annotation every time TypeScript can determine the type correctly.

I studied how to type function parameters and return values in TypeScript. Function parameters can have their types specified after their names. For example, ‘function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b; }’. This means that both parameters are expected to be numbers. A return type can also be specified after the closing parenthesis of the function parameters. For example, ‘function add(a: number, b: number): number { return a + b; }’. The final number indicates that the function should return a number. A function that does not return a value can use the void type, such as ‘function printMessage(message: string): void { console.log(message); }’.

I learned about optional parameters in TypeScript. An optional parameter is marked with a question mark after its name. For example, ‘function greet(name?: string) { }’. This means that the name parameter may be provided, but it is not required. Optional parameters are useful when a function can work with or without a particular piece of information.

I studied union types. A union type allows a variable or parameter to accept more than one type. The vertical bar symbol | is used to create a union. For example, ‘let id: string | number;’ means that id can contain either a string or a number. This can also be used with function parameters, such as ‘function printId(id: string | number) { console.log(id); }’. Union types are useful when a value can legitimately have different possible types.

I learned about type aliases. A type alias allows a new name to be created for a type. For example, ‘type ID = string | number;’. After creating this type, it can be used in other parts of the program, such as ‘let userId: ID;’. Type aliases can make complex types easier to read and reuse. They can also be used to describe object structures. For example, ‘type Student = { name: string; age: number; };’.

I studied interfaces. An interface describes the structure that an object should have. For example, ‘interface Student { name: string; age: number; course: string; }’. An object using this interface must contain the required properties with the correct types. For example, ‘const student: Student = { name: ‘Denise’, age: 20, course: ‘Software Engineering’ };’. If a required property is missing or has the wrong type, TypeScript reports an error.

I learned about optional properties in interfaces. An optional property is created by placing a question mark after the property name. For example, ‘interface Student { name: string; age: number; course?: string; }’. In this example, name and age are required, but course is optional. Therefore, an object such as ‘const student: Student = { name: ‘Denise’, age: 20 };’ is valid even though it does not contain course.

I studied the difference between interfaces and type aliases. Both can be used to describe the structure of objects. For example, an interface can be written as ‘interface User { name: string; age: number; }’, while a type alias can be written as ‘type User = { name: string; age: number; };’. Both can then be used to type objects. Type aliases are also useful for representing union types such as ‘type ID = string | number;’. Interfaces are commonly used when describing the structure of objects, while type aliases can represent a wider variety of types.

I learned about arrays in TypeScript. Arrays can be given a specific element type by using the type followed by square brackets. For example, ‘let numbers: number[] = [1, 2, 3];’ means the array should contain numbers, while ‘let names: string[] = [‘John’, ‘Mary’];’ means the array should contain strings. Arrays can also contain objects defined using an interface. For example, if a Student interface has been created, ‘let students: Student[] = [student1, student2];’ means the array contains Student objects.

I studied generics at a basic level. Generics allow functions, interfaces, and other structures to work with different types while maintaining type safety. A generic is commonly represented using a letter such as T. For example, ‘function identity(value: T): T { return value; }’. The function can be used with a number, such as ‘identity(10);’, or a string, such as ‘identity(‘Hello’);’. TypeScript can often determine the type automatically, so ‘identity(10);’ and ‘identity(‘Hello’);’ can also be used.

I learned that generics are useful because they allow reusable code to work with different types without giving up type checking. For example, ‘function getFirst(items: T[]): T { return items[0]; }’ can work with an array of numbers or an array of strings. When it receives numbers, TypeScript understands that the returned value is a number. When it receives strings, TypeScript understands that the returned value is a string.

I studied type narrowing in TypeScript. Type narrowing means checking the type of a value before using it in a way that requires a particular type. For example, if a function accepts a value of type ‘string | number’, TypeScript does not initially know which type it contains. Using ‘typeof’ allows the program to check the type. For example, ‘function printValue(value: string | number) { if (typeof value === ‘string’) { console.log(value.toUpperCase()); } else { console.log(value.toFixed(2)); } }’. After the typeof check, TypeScript understands that value is a string inside the first block and a number inside the second block.

I learned how to read TypeScript error messages without panicking. TypeScript error messages usually provide information about what went wrong, where the problem occurred, what type was expected, and what type was actually provided. For example, an error such as ‘Type ‘string’ is not assignable to type ‘number’’ means that TypeScript expected a number but received a string. If a function expects a number parameter and I call it using a string, TypeScript can show an error such as ‘Argument of type ‘string’ is not assignable to parameter of type ‘number’’. Reading the error carefully helps identify the exact part of the code that needs to be corrected.

I also learned that TypeScript errors can occur when an object does not follow an interface or type definition. For example, if an interface contains name, age, and course as required properties, creating an object without course will result in an error indicating that the course property is missing. This helps ensure that objects follow the structure defined by the programmer.

Overall, Week 3 helped me move beyond the basic JavaScript concepts studied in Week 2. I learned how to write shorter functions using arrow functions, pass functions as callbacks, use default and rest parameters, use the spread operator, work with advanced array methods, handle asynchronous operations using Promises and async/await, manipulate HTML using the DOM, respond to events, handle forms, and organize code using modules. I also learned the foundations of TypeScript, including why types are useful, type annotations, type inference, function parameter and return types, optional properties, union types, interfaces, type aliases, arrays, generics, type narrowing, and how to understand TypeScript errors. These concepts provide a foundation for writing larger, more organized, interactive, and type-safe JavaScript applications.